---
// Lesson 11.1e — Create PT: Build Day 4 + List Check
// hybrid CPT: final freeze day + the requirements audit ritual.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>11.1e — Create PT: Build Day 4 + List Check</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Last build day before the writing/recording days
    (<a href="11.2a.html">11.2a</a>). Today: freeze, audit, and the
    ritual this whole year trained you for — the requirements
    checklist, done out loud, with witnesses. A PT that passes the
    audit twice cannot be surprised on submission day.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Audit Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The audit ritual — partner swap, read-aloud
    verification. One of you points, one of you checks the rubric
    language:</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Evidence, on screen or in code</th><th>Pass?</th></tr>
        <tr><td>List participating in program function</td><td>Where is it added to, removed from, or traversed — IN the behavior path?</td><td>☐</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Procedure with ≥1 parameter</td><td>Name it, show the signature, show the call passing values</td><td>☐</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Algorithm: sequencing + selection + iteration</td><td>Point at one line for each of the three</td><td>☐</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The CALL</td><td>Where does a call trigger the algorithm from the program's actual behavior?</td><td>☐</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Input → behavior → output</td><td>Trace one user input to one visible output</td><td>☐</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Video plan under 60s</td><td>Rehearse with a stopwatch: demo path intact?</td><td>☐</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>Partner's signature on your CPT_PLAN.md, and yours on
    theirs. This is <a href="8.6.html">8.6</a>'s review ritual,
    aimed at College Board instead of your teacher.</p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Freeze + Writing Installments</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Freeze means freeze.</strong> After today: no new
    features, no refactors, no "small improvements." Every change
    after this point risks the demo path — and your WR answers
    must describe the FINAL code, not the aspirational one.</p>

    <p><strong>WR installments (draft all five installments in
    CPT_PLAN.md — <a href="../exam/written-responses.html">prompts here</a>):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>WR1:</strong> the program's purpose, plus
            <strong>one specific example output</strong> and how it
            shows what the program does. Name the actual text or
            number your program produces — "it works" scores
            nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>WR2a:</strong> the algorithm, explained
            (your <a href="11.1c.html">11.1c</a> paragraph)</li>
        <li><strong>WR2b:</strong> the development story —
            describe ONE difficulty you faced and the fix.
            TODAY's bug-hunt findings ARE this answer: write one
            down while you still remember the debugging
            session.</li>
        <li><strong>WR2c:</strong> BOTH halves of the row — the
            list (what it stores, how the program uses it) and the
            <strong>procedure with its parameter</strong>, arguing
            how it manages complexity (your palette class, the
            UnityEvents, the singleton...).</li>
        <li><strong>The video script:</strong> the demo path as a
            numbered shot list.</li>
    </ol>


    <div class="socratic">


        <h3>Socratic: Freeze, Audit, and What You Cut</h3>


        <ol>


            <li>You read the requirements out loud with witnesses. Which requirement was hardest to point at in your own code, and why was that one hard?</li>


            <li>Freezing means something you wanted to build does not get built. What did you cut, and what would it have cost you to keep it?</li>


            <li>The audit passed twice. What would a third person, who had never seen your program, fail to find?</li>


        </ol>


    </div>



    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Pass the six-row audit with a partner's signature</li>
            <li>Explain every requirement by pointing at your own code</li>
            <li>Show all four WR drafts (plus the video script) in the plan file</li>
            <li>Resist new features until submission (the freeze discipline)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">Create PT guide</a> — the audit rows explained</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/written-responses.html">Written Responses</a> — the four prompts</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.2a.html">11.2a — Create PT: Video + PPR</a></p>
</div>`;
